Zexec: a request may add makefiles after the zygote's own, and its child reads them
before it walks a goal. Make resolves what it read into the form it walks once, in the
steps from snap_deps to build_vpath_lists, and refuses a rule defined after that. The
zygote now parks before those steps, so a child reads what the request added and then
runs them once over the whole set. A probe forked at the park point runs them and the
remake of makefiles; when it remade one the zygote starts over with its serve words and
the restart count, else it serves. A request is served when it restates every makefile
the zygote holds and then names more, in the joined or the separated form; any other
option the zygote lacks is refused as before. A recipe still cannot define a rule.
An added makefile that cannot be read fails the request with make's own status, since
the child skips the remake that would have reported it.

